{
  "id": "ep176",
  "duration": 53.004,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 3.805,
      "text": "A tiny frozen moon is painting one of Saturn's rings.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Enceladus - June 28 2007 (54211737180).png",
        "credit": "Kevin M. Gill",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enceladus_-_June_28_2007_(54211737180).png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 1.7,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 1.7,
          "end": 3.8,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 3.805,
      "end": 10.7,
      "text": "Enceladus is only 310 miles wide, yet it is the most reflective body in the Solar System.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Enceladusstripes cassini-edit2.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/JPL/Space Science Institute",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enceladusstripes_cassini-edit2.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 3.8,
          "end": 6.6,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 6.6,
          "end": 10.7,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 10.7,
      "end": 19.27,
      "text": "Because its pristine surface bounces back almost all sunlight, temperatures hover around minus 324 degrees Fahrenheit.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Enceladus southern hemi tectonics.png",
        "credit": "NASA/JPL/Space Science Institute",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enceladus_southern_hemi_tectonics.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 10.7,
          "end": 19.267,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 19.27,
      "end": 25.715,
      "text": "For over two centuries, astronomers had no idea how such a small rock stayed dazzlingly white.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Herschel 40 foot.jpg",
        "credit": "Unknown authorUnknown author",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herschel_40_foot.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 19.267,
          "end": 25.7,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 25.715,
      "end": 33.46,
      "text": "Then in 2005, the Cassini spacecraft caught giant fractures dubbed tiger stripes erupting near its south pole.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Enceladus Tiger Stripes Up Close PIA06247.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/JPL/Space Science Institute",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enceladus_Tiger_Stripes_Up_Close_PIA06247.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 25.7,
          "end": 33.467,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 33.46,
      "end": 39.255,
      "text": "Massive geysers blast water vapor, salts, and organic molecules straight into space.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Jets of Enceladus and shadows (PIA17184).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/Space Science Institute",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jets_of_Enceladus_and_shadows_(PIA17184).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 33.467,
          "end": 39.267,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 39.255,
      "end": 46.655,
      "text": "Measuring the moon's subtle orbital wobble revealed that these jets tap directly into a global liquid ocean.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 39.267,
          "end": 46.667,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 46.655,
      "end": 52.654,
      "text": "Much of this ocean spray falls back as pristine snow, while the rest escapes to build Saturn's E ring.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Enceladus - June 28 2007 (54211737180).png",
        "credit": "Kevin M. Gill",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enceladus_-_June_28_2007_(54211737180).png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 46.667,
          "end": 53.0,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
